#{[#edb/ref 17592186050420 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528} :activity/id #uuid "9be3f59b-09db-52f4-97b2-cfb6afd98870" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060893 :tutorial/content "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060894 :tutorial/content "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)+\\operatorname{sin}(h)\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Sine"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060895 :tutorial/content "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)-\\operatorname{cos}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Cosine"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060896 :tutorial/content "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059951}] [#edb/ref 17592186048777 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017} :activity/id #uuid "313df706-051c-53f8-a9d2-078969d05bee" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065409 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064052 :question/id #uuid "a9671d61-6b85-5d84-b49c-a6443569f6c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 3\\sin{x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\sin x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cos x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064053 :question/id #uuid "4c3342aa-dd1c-566b-9229-164e594c554c" :question/math-academy-id "e-383" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int (-\\csc x\\cot x)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = - \\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (csc⁡xcot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-csc⁡x)+C \\\\  & =csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065020}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064180 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064054 :question/id #uuid "3d2bde25-f8dc-5e62-9999-6593424f0ae2" :question/math-academy-id "e-384" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = \\tan x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm dx = \\sec x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int (2\\sec^2 x + \\sec x \\tan x )\\, \\textrm{d}x& = 2 \\int \\sec^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ \\int \\sec x \\tan x \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=2\\tan x + \\sec x + C.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant Squared"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064055 :question/id #uuid "c2c57145-3988-5ce8-b13f-114bba6964f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-385" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\csc{x}(\\csc{x}+3\\cot{x} )\\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\csc^2 x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int \\csc x \\cot x \\, \\textrm dx = -\\csc x + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡x(csc⁡x+3cot⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x+3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-cot⁡x-3csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064392}] [#edb/ref 17592186050789 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065168} :activity/id #uuid "43bcaf37-4f35-5441-9262-198383c30f89" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065335 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate trigonometric functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\sin (2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\sin u, \\qquad u = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{cos}(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064125 :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064126 :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+3)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064405}] [#edb/ref 17592186049592 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167625} :activity/id #uuid "646099ed-0bc0-5178-b252-fcbaf49d8855" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168069 :tutorial/content "An **ordinary differential equation** is an equation that involves a function with one variable (usually denoted as $y$), its derivatives, and an independent variable (usually denoted as $x$).\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}-3xy^2 = y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis an ordinary differential equation (ODE). \n\n**Watch out!** Here, the function $y=y(x)$ actually depends on the independent variable $x.$\n\nThe **order of an ordinary differential equation** is the order of the highest derivative in the equation. \n\nIn our example above, the highest derivative is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich has order $2.$ Therefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166651 :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166653 :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168084 :tutorial/content "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be **autonomous** if it can be written in the form \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),\n$$\n\nor equivalently, \n\n$$\ny'=f(y).\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -5y^2\n$$\n\nis autonomous, while the equation \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{y+2}{x^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* autonomous since the expression for $y'$ on the right-hand side contains the independent variable $x$ explicitly, outside the function $y=y(x).$" :tutorial/title "Autonomous Ordinary Differential Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168085 :tutorial/content "A first-order **initial value problem** (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}y(e)=-2}\n$$\n\nis a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}\n$$\n\ntogether with the initial condition \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} y(e)=-2}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "First-Order Initial Value Problems"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167652}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166999}]}
